{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_014jbBZ7R1nM7iwyYE8WTKKq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01ABX926ZLw7SWMxMYeMB7Qr","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1 reacts to R2's review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) in a peer review roundtable. You previously reviewed the paper \"Per-Test Classification for Code Execution Simulation\" focusing on **methods and experiments**, and gave it a **Weak Reject (3/6)**.\n\n## Your Original Review (R1)\n\n### Summary\nThis paper proposes reformulating execution-free code patch verification from generation-based (CWM) to per-test binary classification (pass/fail). Through controlled experiments at 4B/8B scales on function-level and repo-level benchmarks, the authors claim a complexity-dependent advantage for classification.\n\n### Key Strengths You Noted\n- S1: Careful controlled comparison design (same models, LoRA, data, seeds)\n- S2: Appropriate statistical methodology (clustered bootstrap, TOST, ICC)\n- S3: Honest negative results (p≈0.06, p=0.11, majority voting hurts)\n- S4: Oracle framework with testable predictions\n- S5: Comprehensive ablations\n- S6: Reproducibility infrastructure\n\n### Key Weaknesses You Noted\n- W1: Repo-level CWM comparison is fundamentally confounded (implementation failure vs formulation failure)\n- W2: Single-seed repo-level comparisons insufficient\n- W3: CWM 8B \"degradation\" may be hyperparameter transfer failure\n- W4: TOST ±2pp margin is arbitrary; mixed-precision seeds introduce confounds\n- W5: Function-level data is synthetic mutations, not real bugs\n- W6: No downstream validation of diagnostic granularity (re-repair pilot p=0.11)\n- W7: DeepSeek cross-architecture reversal undermines generalizability\n\nYour overall score: Soundness 3/5, Empirical Rigor 3/5, Reproducibility 4/5, **Overall 3/6 (Weak Reject)**, Confidence 4/5.\n\n---\n\n## Reviewer 2's Review (R2: Novelty + Related Work)\n\nR2 also gave **Weak Reject (3/6)**.\n\n### R2's Key Strengths\n- S1: Honest framing of a straightforward idea — not overclaiming novelty\n- S2: Related work has 22 references across 5 paragraphs (more developed than initially appeared)\n- S3: Explicit positioning against LEVER and CodeT\n- S4: NLP task-reformulation context is useful\n- S5: Transparent about mixed/negative results\n\n### R2's Key Weaknesses\n- W1: Core idea (per-test binary classification) is technically straightforward — LEVER already trains classifiers for execution correctness\n- W2: CWM comparison is against authors' own LoRA reproduction at 4B/8B, not the original 32B system\n- W3: Missing important related work: PRM vs ORM parallel, fault localization literature, SWE-Judge, RepoExec, CodeScore, LiveCodeBench, deeper VG-Search engagement\n- W4: VG-Search citation deserves deeper engagement as it directly studies verification granularity\n- W5: No discussion of concurrent/recent work on execution-free verification scaling\n\n### R2's Questions\n- Q1: How is per-test CLS meaningfully different from LEVER at per-test level?\n- Q2: Generation-based approaches may require more capacity — 32B CWM was designed for this, 4B LoRA may be unfair\n- Q3: How much CWM repo-level failure is format/training issue vs formulation issue?\n- Q4: PRM vs ORM parallel — per-test advantage in multi-test instances mirrors PRM findings\n- Q5: Without downstream validation, is per-test granularity advantage mostly theoretical?\n\n### R2's Scores\n- Novelty: 2/5\n- Related Work Completeness: 3/5\n- Significance: 3/5\n- Overall: 3/6 (Weak Reject)\n- Confidence: 4/5\n\n---\n\n## Your Task\n\nReact to R2's review. Consider:\n\n1. **Do you agree with R2's novelty assessment (2/5)?** Is the straightforward nature of per-test CLS a fatal flaw or is the systematic comparison itself valuable?\n2. **The PRM vs ORM parallel** — is this a significant missing connection that would change your assessment?\n3. **R2's point about LEVER** — does the existence of LEVER as a prior classifier-based approach reduce the novelty further?\n4. **The \"unfair CWM comparison\" concern** — R2 raises this from a positioning perspective; you raised it from a methods perspective. Do these converge to a stronger criticism?\n5. **Any points where you disagree with R2?**\n6. **Does R2's review change your score or reasoning?**\n\nRespond in 300-500 words with your reactions, highlighting agreements, disagreements, and any score adjustment.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9610,"cache_read_input_tokens":53436,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9610},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5dda3937-f1b2-4375-bd1e-e2d91b9b5207","uuid":"ec6f92d8-d844-43ee-acf6-02b0ae3f334d"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a0e111a0f3e91a6c0","tool_use_id":"toolu_01ABX926ZLw7SWMxMYeMB7Qr","description":"R1 reacts to R2's review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) in a peer review roundtable. You previously reviewed the paper \"Per-Test Classification for Code Execution Simulation\" focusing on **methods and experiments**, and gave it a **Weak Reject (3/6)**.\n\n## Your Original Review (R1)\n\n### Summary\nThis paper proposes reformulating execution-free code patch verification from generation-based (CWM) to per-test binary classification (pass/fail). Through controlled experiments at 4B/8B scales on function-level and repo-level benchmarks, the authors claim a complexity-dependent advantage for classification.\n\n### Key Strengths You Noted\n- S1: Careful controlled comparison design (same models, LoRA, data, seeds)\n- S2: Appropriate statistical methodology (clustered bootstrap, TOST, ICC)\n- S3: Honest negative results (p≈0.06, p=0.11, majority voting hurts)\n- S4: Oracle framework with testable predictions\n- S5: Comprehensive ablations\n- S6: Reproducibility infrastructure\n\n### Key Weaknesses You Noted\n- W1: Repo-level CWM comparison is fundamentally confounded (implementation failure vs formulation failure)\n- W2: Single-seed repo-level comparisons insufficient\n- W3: CWM 8B \"degradation\" may be hyperparameter transfer failure\n- W4: TOST ±2pp margin is arbitrary; mixed-precision seeds introduce confounds\n- W5: Function-level data is synthetic mutations, not real bugs\n- W6: No downstream validation of diagnostic granularity (re-repair pilot p=0.11)\n- W7: DeepSeek cross-architecture reversal undermines generalizability\n\nYour overall score: Soundness 3/5, Empirical Rigor 3/5, Reproducibility 4/5, **Overall 3/6 (Weak Reject)**, Confidence 4/5.\n\n---\n\n## Reviewer 2's Review (R2: Novelty + Related Work)\n\nR2 also gave **Weak Reject (3/6)**.\n\n### R2's Key Strengths\n- S1: Honest framing of a straightforward idea — not overclaiming novelty\n- S2: Related work has 22 references across 5 paragraphs (more developed than initially appeared)\n- S3: Explicit positioning against LEVER and CodeT\n- S4: NLP task-reformulation context is useful\n- S5: Transparent about mixed/negative results\n\n### R2's Key Weaknesses\n- W1: Core idea (per-test binary classification) is technically straightforward — LEVER already trains classifiers for execution correctness\n- W2: CWM comparison is against authors' own LoRA reproduction at 4B/8B, not the original 32B system\n- W3: Missing important related work: PRM vs ORM parallel, fault localization literature, SWE-Judge, RepoExec, CodeScore, LiveCodeBench, deeper VG-Search engagement\n- W4: VG-Search citation deserves deeper engagement as it directly studies verification granularity\n- W5: No discussion of concurrent/recent work on execution-free verification scaling\n\n### R2's Questions\n- Q1: How is per-test CLS meaningfully different from LEVER at per-test level?\n- Q2: Generation-based approaches may require more capacity — 32B CWM was designed for this, 4B LoRA may be unfair\n- Q3: How much CWM repo-level failure is format/training issue vs formulation issue?\n- Q4: PRM vs ORM parallel — per-test advantage in multi-test instances mirrors PRM findings\n- Q5: Without downstream validation, is per-test granularity advantage mostly theoretical?\n\n### R2's Scores\n- Novelty: 2/5\n- Related Work Completeness: 3/5\n- Significance: 3/5\n- Overall: 3/6 (Weak Reject)\n- Confidence: 4/5\n\n---\n\n## Your Task\n\nReact to R2's review. Consider:\n\n1. **Do you agree with R2's novelty assessment (2/5)?** Is the straightforward nature of per-test CLS a fatal flaw or is the systematic comparison itself valuable?\n2. **The PRM vs ORM parallel** — is this a significant missing connection that would change your assessment?\n3. **R2's point about LEVER** — does the existence of LEVER as a prior classifier-based approach reduce the novelty further?\n4. **The \"unfair CWM comparison\" concern** — R2 raises this from a positioning perspective; you raised it from a methods perspective. Do these converge to a stronger criticism?\n5. **Any points where you disagree with R2?**\n6. **Does R2's review change your score or reasoning?**\n\nRespond in 300-500 words with your reactions, highlighting agreements, disagreements, and any score adjustment.\n","uuid":"3e3d3f26-a75a-4c00-a371-bc47158fe68f","session_id":"5dda3937-f1b2-4375-bd1e-e2d91b9b5207"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) in a peer review roundtable. You previously reviewed the paper \"Per-Test Classification for Code Execution Simulation\" focusing on **methods and experiments**, and gave it a **Weak Reject (3/6)**.\n\n## Your Original Review (R1)\n\n### Summary\nThis paper proposes reformulating execution-free code patch verification from generation-based (CWM) to per-test binary classification (pass/fail). Through controlled experiments at 4B/8B scales on function-level and repo-level benchmarks, the authors claim a complexity-dependent advantage for classification.\n\n### Key Strengths You Noted\n- S1: Careful controlled comparison design (same models, LoRA, data, seeds)\n- S2: Appropriate statistical methodology (clustered bootstrap, TOST, ICC)\n- S3: Honest negative results (p≈0.06, p=0.11, majority voting hurts)\n- S4: Oracle framework with testable predictions\n- S5: Comprehensive ablations\n- S6: Reproducibility infrastructure\n\n### Key Weaknesses You Noted\n- W1: Repo-level CWM comparison is fundamentally confounded (implementation failure vs formulation failure)\n- W2: Single-seed repo-level comparisons insufficient\n- W3: CWM 8B \"degradation\" may be hyperparameter transfer failure\n- W4: TOST ±2pp margin is arbitrary; mixed-precision seeds introduce confounds\n- W5: Function-level data is synthetic mutations, not real bugs\n- W6: No downstream validation of diagnostic granularity (re-repair pilot p=0.11)\n- W7: DeepSeek cross-architecture reversal undermines generalizability\n\nYour overall score: Soundness 3/5, Empirical Rigor 3/5, Reproducibility 4/5, **Overall 3/6 (Weak Reject)**, Confidence 4/5.\n\n---\n\n## Reviewer 2's Review (R2: Novelty + Related Work)\n\nR2 also gave **Weak Reject (3/6)**.\n\n### R2's Key Strengths\n- S1: Honest framing of a straightforward idea — not overclaiming novelty\n- S2: Related work has 22 references across 5 paragraphs (more developed than initially appeared)\n- S3: Explicit positioning against LEVER and CodeT\n- S4: NLP task-reformulation context is useful\n- S5: Transparent about mixed/negative results\n\n### R2's Key Weaknesses\n- W1: Core idea (per-test binary classification) is technically straightforward — LEVER already trains classifiers for execution correctness\n- W2: CWM comparison is against authors' own LoRA reproduction at 4B/8B, not the original 32B system\n- W3: Missing important related work: PRM vs ORM parallel, fault localization literature, SWE-Judge, RepoExec, CodeScore, LiveCodeBench, deeper VG-Search engagement\n- W4: VG-Search citation deserves deeper engagement as it directly studies verification granularity\n- W5: No discussion of concurrent/recent work on execution-free verification scaling\n\n### R2's Questions\n- Q1: How is per-test CLS meaningfully different from LEVER at per-test level?\n- Q2: Generation-based approaches may require more capacity — 32B CWM was designed for this, 4B LoRA may be unfair\n- Q3: How much CWM repo-level failure is format/training issue vs formulation issue?\n- Q4: PRM vs ORM parallel — per-test advantage in multi-test instances mirrors PRM findings\n- Q5: Without downstream validation, is per-test granularity advantage mostly theoretical?\n\n### R2's Scores\n- Novelty: 2/5\n- Related Work Completeness: 3/5\n- Significance: 3/5\n- Overall: 3/6 (Weak Reject)\n- Confidence: 4/5\n\n---\n\n## Your Task\n\nReact to R2's review. Consider:\n\n1. **Do you agree with R2's novelty assessment (2/5)?** Is the straightforward nature of per-test CLS a fatal flaw or is the systematic comparison itself valuable?\n2. **The PRM vs ORM parallel** — is this a significant missing connection that would change your assessment?\n3. **R2's point about LEVER** — does the existence of LEVER as a prior classifier-based approach reduce the novelty further?\n4. **The \"unfair CWM comparison\" concern** — R2 raises this from a positioning perspective; you raised it from a methods perspective. Do these converge to a stronger criticism?\n5. **Any points where you disagree with R2?**\n6. **Does R2's review change your score or reasoning?**\n\nRespond in 300-500 words with your reactions, highlighting agreements, disagreements, and any score adjustment.\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01ABX926ZLw7SWMxMYeMB7Qr","session_id":"5dda3937-f1b2-4375-bd1e-e2d91b9b5207","uuid":"8c22a43d-686a-4bcd-b9ca-333b9906f7a5","timestamp":"2026-05-16T08:26:08.765Z"}
