{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ABX926ZLw7SWMxMYeMB7Qr","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have a thorough understanding of the paper. Let me compose my R1 reaction to R2's review.\n\n---\n\n## R1 Reaction to R2's Review\n\n**On novelty (R2 W1, Q1): I partially agree but think R2 overstates the problem.** R2 is right that per-test binary classification is not a conceptual leap -- anyone familiar with LEVER could propose it. But I give the authors more credit than R2 does for the *controlled comparison methodology*. The contribution is less \"here is a new formulation\" and more \"here is rigorous evidence that formulation choice matters, and here is when it matters.\" Novelty 2/5 feels slightly harsh; I would say 2.5/5. That said, R2's LEVER point is well-taken: the paper's related work (Section 3, paragraph 3) acknowledges LEVER but under-explains what is genuinely new beyond per-test granularity. The distinction between candidate-level re-ranking (LEVER) and per-test diagnostic verdicts is real but thin.\n\n**On the PRM vs ORM parallel (R2 Q4): This is a significant omission I missed.** R2 identifies an important structural analogy: per-test classification gaining advantage on multi-test instances mirrors process reward models outperforming outcome reward models on multi-step problems. The oracle framework in Section 3.3 essentially re-derives this insight domain-specifically. Connecting to the PRM literature would strengthen the theoretical grounding considerably and is a genuine gap in positioning. I did not catch this in my original review, and it slightly increases my concern about incomplete framing.\n\n**On the CWM comparison (convergence with R2 W2, Q2): Yes, these converge.** My W1 (confounded repo-level comparison) and R2's point about comparing against a 4B/8B LoRA reproduction rather than the original 32B CWM are two facets of the same problem. From my methods angle: we cannot distinguish implementation failure from formulation failure. From R2's positioning angle: the paper implicitly claims generation is fundamentally limited, but the comparison handicaps generation by using far smaller models than CWM was designed for. Together, this is the paper's most serious weakness. The authors do caveat this (experiments.tex line 14 explicitly states their CWM is \"not a reproduction of the 32B Code World Model\"), but the paper's narrative still leans toward formulation-level conclusions from capacity-limited evidence.\n\n**Where I disagree with R2:** R2's missing-related-work list (SWE-Judge, RepoExec, CodeScore, LiveCodeBench) is valid but not as damaging as R2 implies. The paper has 22 references and covers the main threads. Missing PRM/ORM and fault localization literature is more consequential than missing individual benchmarks. I also disagree that VG-Search needs \"deeper engagement\" -- the current one-sentence treatment is adequate given VG-Search studies a different axis (granularity for test-time scaling, not formulation comparison).\n\n**Score adjustment:** R2's review reinforces my concerns without introducing anything that fundamentally changes my assessment. The PRM/ORM omission is notable but addressable in revision. The CWM comparison convergence confirms it as the central weakness. I maintain **3/6 (Weak Reject)**, though I would note that a revision addressing the CWM confound (e.g., comparing against a stronger generation baseline or the original 32B model) and adding the PRM connection could move me to a 4."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a0e111a0f3e91a6c0 (use SendMessage with to: 'a0e111a0f3e91a6c0' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 27069\ntool_uses: 5\nduration_ms: 42765</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5dda3937-f1b2-4375-bd1e-e2d91b9b5207","uuid":"c61fd43a-6e65-48b8-8748-ddd7ade0c9c1","timestamp":"2026-05-16T08:26:51.531Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) in a peer review roundtable. You previously reviewed the paper \"Per-Test Classification for Code Execution Simulation\" focusing on **methods and experiments**, and gave it a **Weak Reject (3/6)**.\n\n## Your Original Review (R1)\n\n### Summary\nThis paper proposes reformulating execution-free code patch verification from generation-based (CWM) to per-test binary classification (pass/fail). Through controlled experiments at 4B/8B scales on function-level and repo-level benchmarks, the authors claim a complexity-dependent advantage for classification.\n\n### Key Strengths You Noted\n- S1: Careful controlled comparison design (same models, LoRA, data, seeds)\n- S2: Appropriate statistical methodology (clustered bootstrap, TOST, ICC)\n- S3: Honest negative results (p≈0.06, p=0.11, majority voting hurts)\n- S4: Oracle framework with testable predictions\n- S5: Comprehensive ablations\n- S6: Reproducibility infrastructure\n\n### Key Weaknesses You Noted\n- W1: Repo-level CWM comparison is fundamentally confounded (implementation failure vs formulation failure)\n- W2: Single-seed repo-level comparisons insufficient\n- W3: CWM 8B \"degradation\" may be hyperparameter transfer failure\n- W4: TOST ±2pp margin is arbitrary; mixed-precision seeds introduce confounds\n- W5: Function-level data is synthetic mutations, not real bugs\n- W6: No downstream validation of diagnostic granularity (re-repair pilot p=0.11)\n- W7: DeepSeek cross-architecture reversal undermines generalizability\n\nYour overall score: Soundness 3/5, Empirical Rigor 3/5, Reproducibility 4/5, **Overall 3/6 (Weak Reject)**, Confidence 4/5.\n\n---\n\n## Reviewer 2's Review (R2: Novelty + Related Work)\n\nR2 also gave **Weak Reject (3/6)**.\n\n### R2's Key Strengths\n- S1: Honest framing of a straightforward idea — not overclaiming novelty\n- S2: Related work has 22 references across 5 paragraphs (more developed than initially appeared)\n- S3: Explicit positioning against LEVER and CodeT\n- S4: NLP task-reformulation context is useful\n- S5: Transparent about mixed/negative results\n\n### R2's Key Weaknesses\n- W1: Core idea (per-test binary classification) is technically straightforward — LEVER already trains classifiers for execution correctness\n- W2: CWM comparison is against authors' own LoRA reproduction at 4B/8B, not the original 32B system\n- W3: Missing important related work: PRM vs ORM parallel, fault localization literature, SWE-Judge, RepoExec, CodeScore, LiveCodeBench, deeper VG-Search engagement\n- W4: VG-Search citation deserves deeper engagement as it directly studies verification granularity\n- W5: No discussion of concurrent/recent work on execution-free verification scaling\n\n### R2's Questions\n- Q1: How is per-test CLS meaningfully different from LEVER at per-test level?\n- Q2: Generation-based approaches may require more capacity — 32B CWM was designed for this, 4B LoRA may be unfair\n- Q3: How much CWM repo-level failure is format/training issue vs formulation issue?\n- Q4: PRM vs ORM parallel — per-test advantage in multi-test instances mirrors PRM findings\n- Q5: Without downstream validation, is per-test granularity advantage mostly theoretical?\n\n### R2's Scores\n- Novelty: 2/5\n- Related Work Completeness: 3/5\n- Significance: 3/5\n- Overall: 3/6 (Weak Reject)\n- Confidence: 4/5\n\n---\n\n## Your Task\n\nReact to R2's review. Consider:\n\n1. **Do you agree with R2's novelty assessment (2/5)?** Is the straightforward nature of per-test CLS a fatal flaw or is the systematic comparison itself valuable?\n2. **The PRM vs ORM parallel** — is this a significant missing connection that would change your assessment?\n3. **R2's point about LEVER** — does the existence of LEVER as a prior classifier-based approach reduce the novelty further?\n4. **The \"unfair CWM comparison\" concern** — R2 raises this from a positioning perspective; you raised it from a methods perspective. Do these converge to a stronger criticism?\n5. **Any points where you disagree with R2?**\n6. **Does R2's review change your score or reasoning?**\n\nRespond in 300-500 words with your reactions, highlighting agreements, disagreements, and any score adjustment.\n","agentId":"a0e111a0f3e91a6c0","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have a thorough understanding of the paper. Let me compose my R1 reaction to R2's review.\n\n---\n\n## R1 Reaction to R2's Review\n\n**On novelty (R2 W1, Q1): I partially agree but think R2 overstates the problem.** R2 is right that per-test binary classification is not a conceptual leap -- anyone familiar with LEVER could propose it. But I give the authors more credit than R2 does for the *controlled comparison methodology*. The contribution is less \"here is a new formulation\" and more \"here is rigorous evidence that formulation choice matters, and here is when it matters.\" Novelty 2/5 feels slightly harsh; I would say 2.5/5. That said, R2's LEVER point is well-taken: the paper's related work (Section 3, paragraph 3) acknowledges LEVER but under-explains what is genuinely new beyond per-test granularity. The distinction between candidate-level re-ranking (LEVER) and per-test diagnostic verdicts is real but thin.\n\n**On the PRM vs ORM parallel (R2 Q4): This is a significant omission I missed.** R2 identifies an important structural analogy: per-test classification gaining advantage on multi-test instances mirrors process reward models outperforming outcome reward models on multi-step problems. The oracle framework in Section 3.3 essentially re-derives this insight domain-specifically. Connecting to the PRM literature would strengthen the theoretical grounding considerably and is a genuine gap in positioning. I did not catch this in my original review, and it slightly increases my concern about incomplete framing.\n\n**On the CWM comparison (convergence with R2 W2, Q2): Yes, these converge.** My W1 (confounded repo-level comparison) and R2's point about comparing against a 4B/8B LoRA reproduction rather than the original 32B CWM are two facets of the same problem. From my methods angle: we cannot distinguish implementation failure from formulation failure. From R2's positioning angle: the paper implicitly claims generation is fundamentally limited, but the comparison handicaps generation by using far smaller models than CWM was designed for. Together, this is the paper's most serious weakness. The authors do caveat this (experiments.tex line 14 explicitly states their CWM is \"not a reproduction of the 32B Code World Model\"), but the paper's narrative still leans toward formulation-level conclusions from capacity-limited evidence.\n\n**Where I disagree with R2:** R2's missing-related-work list (SWE-Judge, RepoExec, CodeScore, LiveCodeBench) is valid but not as damaging as R2 implies. The paper has 22 references and covers the main threads. Missing PRM/ORM and fault localization literature is more consequential than missing individual benchmarks. I also disagree that VG-Search needs \"deeper engagement\" -- the current one-sentence treatment is adequate given VG-Search studies a different axis (granularity for test-time scaling, not formulation comparison).\n\n**Score adjustment:** R2's review reinforces my concerns without introducing anything that fundamentally changes my assessment. The PRM/ORM omission is notable but addressable in revision. The CWM comparison convergence confirms it as the central weakness. I maintain **3/6 (Weak Reject)**, though I would note that a revision addressing the CWM confound (e.g., comparing against a stronger generation baseline or the original 32B model) and adding the PRM connection could move me to a 4."}],"totalDurationMs":42765,"totalTokens":27069,"totalToolUseCount":5,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2701,"cache_read_input_tokens":23549,"output_tokens":818,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2701},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":818,"cache_read_input_tokens":23549,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2701,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2701,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":4,"searchCount":0,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":1}}}
